Ladybird’s PNG encoder now leaves the color values of an opaque pixel
as they are and zeroes those of a fully transparent one, dividing by
alpha only for a translucent pixel, and passes a row that’s opaque
throughout without unpremultiplying it at all. Gecko’s nsPNGEncoder::
ConvertHostARGBRow() takes the same shortcuts for each pixel and divides
with the same rounding. Blink encodes through Skia’s SkPngEncoderBase,
which converts each premultiplied row with SkConvertPixels(), and skips
the conversion for an image whose alpha type is opaque. WebKit’s Cocoa
port leaves the conversion to CoreGraphics, through ImageUtilitiesCG’s
call to CGImageDestinationAddImage(). From reading the source.
